#0
GAIN
Usage: gain
 
In order to advance in levels on PhoenixMUD, you must go to your
guildmaster and type 'GAIN'.   You will know when you need to see
a guildmaster because your experience point display will show a
negative number.  Not all guildmasters will train all classes or
levels, so you must find the proper one.  You can earn enough exp-
erience for up to two levels before you must go see a guildmaster
and gain.  It is advisable, however, to gain as soon as you have 
enough experience points for that level.
 
  The following things (and only the following things) affect the
hp, mana, and movement points you get when you gain a level:
  - Your class
  - Your race
  - Your int and wis (affects mana points)
  - Your con (affects hitpoints)
  - Your dex (affects movement points)
  - A random number
 
To see the relative hp and mana bonuses for each race, see help races.
 
Non-remorts under level 40 receive the following bonuses when they gain:
    Levels  1-15: +15 to int/wis/dex/con
    Levels 16-30: +10 to int/wis/dex/con
    Levels 31-40:  +5 to int/wis/dex/con
#0
TAKE GET

"Get" and "take" are exactly the same and can be used interchangeably.

Usage: get | take <object>
       get | take all [object]
       get | take all all.<object>
       get | take all.<object> all.<object>

If you find something interesting on the ground, you may use 'get' to pick
it up.  'get' can also be used to extract items from containers.  You can now
get items from equipment you are wearing without having to remove the piece
of equipment first.  For example, get bread backpack.

Examples:

  > get sword corpse
  > get all corpse
  > get all all.bag
  > get all.bread all.bag

See also: DROP, PUT
#0
GIVE

Usage: give <object> <victim>
       give <number> coins <victim>

If you feel benevolent, you may give some stuff to someone in need.

  > give all ras
  > give bread doctor
  > give all.bronze mirlen
  > give 40000 coins nia
#0
GOLD

Usage: gold

Displays how much gold you are carrying and have in the bank.

See also: BALANCE, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAW
#0
GRAB HOLD

Usage: grab <object>
       hold <object>

For getting a good grip on something; a torch, for example.

Examples:

  > hold torch
  > grab stone

See also: EQUIPMENT, REMOVE, WEAR, WIELD
#0
GROUP

Usage: group [player]
       group all

GROUP with no arguments shows the status of your group.  GROUP <player>
adds or removes <player> from your group.  GROUP ALL adds everyone who is
following you to your group.

If you are a group member, then you will share experience points for killing
monsters equally among the group.  Experience points will only be shared among
group members that are in the same location, at the time of the killing
blow.  Sharing is regardless of whether the group members have been
participating in the fight.

Group with no argument shows a list of people with whom you are grouped.
"group all" groups all people who are following you.

To make a group, a "leader" must be selected.  All members of the group
must then "follow" the leader using the follow command.  The leader should
NOT follow anyone at all!  The leader can now toggle group membership for
each player in the group, by using "group <player_name>".  The leader
should remember to include himself in the group.

Example:

  Quifael and Redferne wishes to group with Dragon.

  Quifael : "follow dragon"
  Redferne: "follow dragon"
  (Dragon should be following no one but himself)
  Dragon  : "group dragon"
  Dragon  : "group redferne"
  Dragon  : "group quifael"

Later when Dragon get upset at Quifael (because he is sleeping all the time)
he types : "Group Quifael" to kick Quifael out of the group.

See also: EXPERIENCE, UNGROUP, XP, GREPORT, SPLIT, FOLLOW, GWHO, GSAY
#0
GSAY GTELL

Usage: gsay <message>
       gtell <message>

Tell something to all the members of your group, even if they're not in the
room.

See also: GROUP, TELL, SAY, GWHO
#0
GWHO
 
This command displays all the people on the mud who are grouped
or not grouped, and who the leader of the group is.
 
See Also: WHO GROUP
#0
HELP

Usage: help [command]
help 2 [command]

Help searches for a partial match of the entered word, including any
spaces that may follow the word.  Help with a number before the argument
will show help on an entry that has the same or similar name.  Help alone
will give a list of the most common commands in the game.

Examples:

> help magic mis
will find the help text for the magic missile spell

> help mag
will match 'magic user' or 'read magic' depending on the first
occurence in the help file.

> help 2 mag
will match the second occurence of 'mag' in the help file

See Also: COMMANDS, SOCIALS, INDEX
#0
HOUSE

The HOUSE command is used to add or remove guests from your house's guest
list.  You must be standing in your house to use this command.  Only the
primary owner of a house (not guests) may change the guest list using the
HOUSE command.

Usage: house [<player name>]

Typing 'house' with no arguments gives a list of the people currently on
your house's guest list.  Typing 'house' with the name of a player will add
that player to your guest list if the player is not on the list, or will
remove the player from the guest list if the player is already on the list.
The player specified must be in the player database for the MUD, although
he or she does not necessarily have to be logged on at the time.

Guests of your house will be able to enter your house until you remove them
from the guest list.

See also: HOUSES
#0
INDEX

Usage: index <letter|phrase>

The INDEX command will list all help entries which begin with the letter
specified or the phrase specified.  Use the HELP command to then view the
appropriate entry you wish to receive more information on.

Example:
> index h
> index so

See Also: HELP COMMANDS
#0
INFO NEWS POLICY VERSION

These commands show you important information about PhoenixMUD.  It is a good
idea to read them every once in a while.

   info     - useful information about the game for beginners
   news     - latest changes to the code and areas
   policy   - policies on topics like player killing
   credits  - the people who made PhoenixMUD possible!
   version  - displays the current version number
#0
INFOBAR SCOREBAR METER RESIZE REDRAW ASCII

To summarize, the infobar is a small section of lines on your screen
reserved for displaying important data, such as you health, endurance,
mana, gold, experience, the room you are in, and exits.  Additionally,
there is another window you can open up to give you even more data, such
as your abilities, combat, level, practices, etc.  Here are the related
commands:

INFOBAR      - turns the basic infobar on. (takes four lines of your screen)
SCOREBAR     - turns the extended bar on, but you must have the infobar on
               for this to display.  takes 6 lines from your screen. Note
               that while this bar is on, might not be able to scroll
               back completely. It is not meant to be used fulltime as the
               infobar is, but rather as a replacement for SCORE if you
               use the bar.  Useful while trying different equipment.
METER        - turns on an opponent health graph.  This will appear inside
               the basic infobar while you are fighting.  Again, this requires
               that you have the infobar on to be functional.
REDRAW       - Redraws your screen if it gets garbled.  This tends to happen
               sometimes when there is a lot of scrolling taking place or
               when you are typing while something is being written to the
               screen.
RESIZE       - Changes your screen-length.  This has several effects.  If
               you are using the infobar, then it allows you to use your
               entire screen.  Even if you don't use the infobar, you should
               set this value appropriately so the continue prompts will
               appear at appropriate places on your screen.  Note that setting
	       the resize value for your screen will also affect how much info
	       is displayed per page to your screen when you view such things as
               large help files, board messages, etc.
ASCII        - For those terminals which support box-drawing characters,
               this will make your infobar and opponent health graph look
               nicer.  You just have to experiment and see if it works for
               you.  You need to use redraw after toggeling this.

Some notes:  the infobar will not work correctly with MUD clients like
TinyFugue, etc.  Your terminal must be capable of handling vt102 scroll
region codes.  Set your term to vt100 or vt102.  This might require you to
turn your color off on some systems, as some emulators will not decode both
ANSI color and vt102 sequences.  You may need to experiment with various
combinations to get these features to work.  Since they are platform
specific, you're mileage may vary.

See Also: PROMPT
#0
