#0
ABBREVIATIONS

When referring to a player, mobile, or object in the game, it is not
necessary to use the full name.  For example, if you wanted to tell to
Aleksandr, you could use the following command:

  > tell aleks hiya

Similarly, if you wanted to look at a monster named biguglymob, you could
type:

  > look big

If you have trouble using a mob, obj, or player name for any reason,
simply use the following syntax: (A period after the full name).

  > tell tell how are you?
  You tell Tellurius, 'How are you?'
  > tell telly. how are you?
  You tell Telly, 'How are you?'

See Also: TELL
#0
AESIR

An extremely rare race, contact with them is generally unrecorded as they
are known to help those on the brink of death.
Their empathy and mystic powers have saved the life of many an injured
traveller.  On very rare occasions they have reportedly saved caravans from
orc, ogre, troll and other such raiding parties.  On these very rare
occasionas, the Aesir have been reported to all be of a height of
approximately 6 foot, with blonde hair, and perfect appearance.  They
emanate a holy aura which causes the raiding party to flee more often than
not, but on the odd occasion that a battle has ensued, the Aesir has been
seen to wield magical powers without any effort whatsoever.

See also: RACES REMORT
#0
ANTI-PALADIN

Anti-Paladins are similar to Paladins, only they worship evil gods rather
than good ones.  Anti-Paladins are holy warriors, but in a twisted, evil 
way.  Strong fighters, Anti-Paladins can hold their own against most other
fighting classes.  Combined with their clerical powers granted by their evil
masters, Anti-Paladins make a force to be reckoned with.  Whereas Paladin's
clerical powers are generally limited to healing and curative spells,
Anti-Paladin's magic is limited to causing pain and suffering.  Anti-Paladins
will lose their special abilities if they stray from their evil natures.

See Also: CLASSES
#0
ASSASSIN

Whilst similar to the thief in many ways, the assassins far superior skills
in both combat and magic mean their talent comes at a very high premium to
those who wish to enroll their service.  Trained in the denizens where most
fear to tread, their soulless approach to their task at hand make them
invaluable in the service of the powerbrokers of the world.  Relying on
their ability to get in and get their job done in a very quick manor, their
combat training in fighting and magic is based around offense and stealth.
An assassin, unlike the lesser thief has no honor, as such an act would
mean certain death for an individual in such a cutthroat occupation.

See also: CLASSES REMORT
#0
AVIAN

Avians are a race of bird-like humanoids.  Avians share many traits of
birds, including having wings and the power of flight.  Avians resemble
slim, tall humans with large feathery wings.  Their heads also closely
resemble a birds, with small darting eyes and a sharp beak.

Avians make their homes in areas where there are deep canyons and cliffs.
They create holes in the cliff walls which they can fly into and out of.
Individual holes can be quite large and elaborate on the inside.  In
large avian villages, entire canyon walls can be dotted with entrances
to avian homes.

Avians are extremely intelligent and thus make excellent magic users. Like
a bird, their bones are hollow, making them very weak and poor warriors.
An avian would never attempt a fighting profession, instead preferring
to use their high intelligence and power of flight to defeat an enemy.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
BARBARIAN

Barbarians are a variety of warriors, prizing power and aggression over
skill and discipline.  Where a warrior aims blows with cunning precision,
the barbarian smashes through his enemy's defenses.  Where a warrior
pauses to defend against an attack, the barbarian attacks some more.  The
barbarian's motto is: "The best defense is a good offense," and their
skills and techniques reflect this.  Like warriors, barbarians have no
knowledge of magic.

See Also: CLASSES
#0
BARD

Bards are a group of individuals that can't be clearly classed as
anything other than jacks-of-all-trades, knowing something of everything
but mastering nothing.  Bards have some thief-like skills, although
they're not thieves; they can swing a sword like a warrior, but they're
definitely not warriors; they know some tricks of the magic users, but
they're not sorcerers.  Their magics tend to be more of trickery or
deception than of all-out destruction, although they do have some
offensive spells.

See Also: CLASSES
#0
CIRCLEMUD CREDITS PHOENIXMUD

This help file will be coming shortly.  Stay tuned.
For now please just type credits, version, and areas.
#0
CLASSES

There are 11 regular classes available to players on Phoenix MUD.
Your character's class is your character's profession.  It determines 
what skills and spells he/she gets and how good he/she is with 
certain things, like fighting and casting spells.

The classes are: Magic User, Warrior, Thief, Cleric, Barbarian, Bard, 
Paladin, Anti-Paladin, Druid, Ranger, Monk.

See Also: MAGIC-USER WARRIOR THIEF CLERIC BARBARIAN BARD PALADIN
ANTI-PALADIN DRUID RANGER MONK
#0
CLERIC

Clerics are priests and shamans, drawing their magic from the gods of the
world.  Their spells are generally protective or curative in nature,
making them invaluable to the sickly or injured, and their services are
highly prized amongst adventurers.  While lacking in combat skills,
clerics are by no means defenseless, being capable fighters in their own
right, and while their spells generally won't injure an opponent, they
WILL serve to keep the cleric alive longer.

See also: CLASSES
#0
CREDITS

Usage: credits

This command will display the credits for the game--those who helped build,
code, and administer in any significant way.  This is a purely informational
command.
#0
Darkelf Darkelves Dark-Elf

The dark elves are a feared race of subterranean elves, renowned 
for their cruelty and xenophobia.  They are shorter than humans and built 
much slimmer, and move with an easy grace.  Many of the races that live 
on the surface hate and despise the dark elves for their occasional 
surface raids; not surprisingly, the other subterranean races hate them 
too, since they have to put up with attacks much more frequently.

Dark elves are highly intelligent.  Their slight build makes them 
physically weaker than the average human, but they still make fierce 
warriors.  Centuries of living underground have given them the ability to 
see in total darkness, making them very dangerous indeed when they're in 
their native environment.  This aversion to the sun has also made them 
extremely sensitive to light; sunlight is VERY harmful to them.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
DEVA

Hand picked by the churches to be their foremost representatives, these
healers are looked upon by the gods of goodness with great favour.  Whilst
maintaining many of the abilities of the cleric, the eldest of the Deva
have begun to uncover some of the greater powers the strong link to their
gods allow, which they gladly pass on to willing students.  The deva
however is not a mere healer like their cleric counterpart and are not
restricted to blunt weapons, often choosing bladed weapons in close quarter
combat.  They however value the purity of life above all else, only
choosing combat as a last resort.

See also: CLASSES REMORT
#0
DRACONIAN

Originally the product of the dragon Ancalador crossbreeding with a human,
they are an extremely aggressive and powerful race.  Draconian socieities
are militaristic and highly intensive in finding suitably talented members
of their society.  Due to their dragon heritage, draconians have a very
long life span, are also naturally adept at magic.  The blood within their
veins burns red hot much like their lineage, and many a draconian has been
seen walking out of a fire almost unharmed by the intense heat.  They do
however anger quickly and are known to show a distinct lack of respect to
the other races.

See also: RACES REMORT
#0
DRUID

Druids are clerics who are attuned to nature.  Druids spend all of their
time in the outdoors, away from large cities.  They are devoted to 
protecting the woods and wildlife at all costs.  Druids are kin to rangers,
but follow their ideals in more peaceful ways.  Druidic spells reflect the
caster's love for mother nature and most draw their power from the elements.
Druids, like the clerical cousins, do not use edged weapons.  Druids have limited
healing abilities as well.  Druids tend to be very powerful at higher levels,
harnessing the awesome forces of nature.

See Also: CLASSES
#0
DWARF

Dwarves are short, stout humanoids who spend much of their time working
with stone.  It is rumoured that Dwarves were born from the living rock
of the earth.  This theory helps to explain their natural affinity for stone.

Dwarves are reknowned stone masons and their skills are highly sought after.
Unfortunately, Dwarves do not get along well with other races, especially
the various elven races.

Dwarves are of short stature, but quite broad in the shoulders, giving them
a natural strength greater than that of humans.  Dwarves spend much of
their time mining for gems deep underground and as a result have developed
a keen sense of infravision.  Dwarves are not known to be of high
intelligence, and thus the magic professions are forbidden to them.  There
are however, several groups of clerics who are granted powers by the
Dwarven gods.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
EDITOR

PhoenixMUD uses a different text editor than most players may be used to from
other MUDs.  The new editor allows greater text editing functionality, including
a rudimentary search and replace feature.  Many of the editor's features may only
be useful to builders who do extensive text editing.

The following commands maybe used while in the text editor.  The command must
be entered on its own line, or it will merely be part of the written text.
Builders will find the /fi command extremely useful, and are advised to use it on
each and every description to provide for the proper length and formatting.

/a         -  aborts editor
/c         -  clears buffer
/d#        -  deletes a line #
/e# <text> -  changes the line at # with <text>
/f         -  formats text
/fi        -  indented formatting of text
/h         -  list text editor commands
/i# <text> -  inserts <text> before line #
/l         -  lists buffer
/n         -  lists buffer with line numbers
/r 'a' 'b' -  replace 1st occurance of text <a> in buffer with text <b>
/ra 'a' 'b'-  replace all occurances of text <a> within buffer with text <b>
              usage: /r[a] 'pattern' 'replacement'
/s         -  saves text

See Also: BOARDS OASIS WRITE MAIL
#0
ELF

The gray elves are a breed of elf devoted to quiet meditation, serious 
study, and higher learning in all its aspects.  They are the most 
paranoid of the elven races, keeping to themselves whenever possible and 
even going so far as to kill outsiders who discover their cities, even 
though they're not inheritantly evil.

Since intense study is a part of life for all gray elves from an early 
age, they are very intelligent and learned on the whole.  Physical 
activity is not promoted, and consequently, they're generally weaker and 
have less endurance than most humans.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
EXPERIENCE XP

Whenever you kill a monster or a player, you earn experience points.  When
enough experience points are earned, you can raise in levels by seeing a 
guildmaster and using the GAIN command.

Things you should know about experience points:

  * When damaging a monster, you get experience points.

  * If you kill a monster of higher level than yourself, you get bonus
    experience points.  If killing a monster of lower level you'll gain
    less experience points
  
  * If you kill the same monster over and over again you will receive
    less experience points.  It is advisable to be as diverse as possible
    in your killings in order to get the maximum number of experience points
    from your kills.

  * If you flee from combat, you will lose experience points.

See also: GROUP SCORE GAIN PRACTICE LEARN
#0
FLAGS

Players sometimes have FLAGS after their names in the WHO list.  These
flags are always in parentheses, not brackets or braces.

  FLAG        MEANING
  ----------- ---------------------------------------------------------
  (AFK)	      Player is away from the keyboard.
  (KILLER)    Player is trying to kill other players.  That means you
              can try to kill him/her without being flagged.
  (PLAGUE !!) Player has the plague! Avoid at all costs!
  (THIEF)     Player is trying to steal from other players.  Fire away.
  (invis)     Player is invisible; you can see him/her because you are
              sensitive to the presence of invisible things.
  (writing)   Player is writing on the board; do not disturb.
  (mailing)   Player is writing mail; do not disturb.
  (deaf)      Player has chosen not to hear shouts.
  (notell)    Player has chosen not to accept tells.
  (notitle)   Player's title privelage has been taken away by the gods.
  (quest)     Player is participating in a quest currently being run by
              the Gods.

See also: NOSHOUT, NOTELL, QUEST, STEAL, INVISIBLE, VISIBLE, AFK
#0
HANDS HANDEDNESS

PhoenixMUD has a true two-handed system.  What this means is that you can
use only your two hands in a realistic manner.  No more wielding a two-
handed weapon, wearing a shield, holding a light, and grabbing some other
item.  You can now only do two of these things at once, unless you are
wielding a two handed weapon in which case you cannot use your other hand
for anything else.  Certain classes also have the ability to wield two
single-handed weapons at the same time.  To compensate for the change, lights
can now be found which can be worn on different parts of your body.

Some examples of hand-use:
A long sword and a shield
A two-handed sword
A long sword and a dagger (for those with Dual Wield)
A long sword and a torch
A long sword and a holdable item
Two holdable items
#0
Halfelf Halfelves Half-Elf

Half Elves are the result of matings between humans and elves, usually gray
elves.  Usually, but not always, the father is human and the mother elven.
Due to their bastardized nature, half elves are not readily accepted by either
humans or elves.  Thus, they tend to become loners, becoming closer to nature
by being rangers or druids.

Half elves are stockier than their elven relatives, but still smaller than
humans.  Their features are generally more delicate and refined than a humans.
Features such as pointed ears and lack of facial hair might distinguish a
half elf from otherwise normal humans.

See Also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
HALFLING

Halflings are a race of small humanoids.  About the same height as dwarves,
the halflings share little else with the dour dwarves.  Halflings prefer
to live outside, often in wooded areas or open fields of lush grass.  They
are accomplished farmers and extremely self-sufficient.  Halflings often
walk around barefoot, their thick leathery soles and hairy feet protecting
them from the elements.

Halflings are an extremely fun loving race, rarely taking up the yoke of
adventuring.  Most halflings prefer to eat and drink and be jovial than
suffer through the hardships of an adventuring life.   Occasionally when
they do go out into the world for adventure, haflings choose to become
thieves, aiding their fellow companions with skills such as picking locks
and sneaking around.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
H_OGRE HALF_OGRE

Half Ogres are the result of interbreeding between ogres and humans.  Parents
are usually human females and male ogres.   Half-ogres are extremely large,
usually reaching about seven feet.   They are extremely strong and as dumb
as they are strong.  

Half ogres are usually shunned by humans at an early age as they begin to
develop strength greater than anyone else.  Half-ogres are almost always
adventurers due to the fact that most do not have the brains necessary to
perform any kind of skilled labor.  All half-ogres choose professions
related to fighting.  Half-ogres usually must take direction from a more
intelligent part member.   Despite this, they are excellent warriors and
a strong asset to any adventuring group.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
H_ORC HALF_ORC

Half-orcs are the result of interbreeding between humans and orcs.  Half-orcs
are conniving, devious creatures.  They are of slightly stockier build than
humans, but often quite a bit shorter, often topping out at little over five
feet tall.

Due to their orcish half, half-orcs are often cruel and evil.  Not generally
accepted in human communities or orcish communities, half-orcs usually only
associate with the lower levels of society.  Lacking any high intelligence,
half-orcs are typically found to be brigands, thieves, and assassins.  Often
they will join up with a more intelligent leader to form an organized band
of thieves and assassins in the larger cities of the realms.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
HOUSES
The current prices for houses are:

First room:	 		1 million coins
Additional rooms:		500k coins each
Running water (fountain):	100k coins
Decorative Furniture		100K
	Includes Cabinets, sofas, chairs, toilets, etc...)
Location move:			put an addition on
Pet				100K
Butler				Free with 6 or more room house.
Portal (one per house)		5,000K coins + 25 quest points

You will be able to specify who has permission to enter your house (up to
10 guest characters, including your multi's) and you may leave stuff in
your house's first room during crashes/reboots.  You will be able to camp
in your home.

Hoarding is not permitted in houses.  Each "save" room may hold no more
than 50 items, and no two items may be the same room with the following
exceptions:
 
Potions (as many as you want)
Wands (as many as you want)
Food items (as many as you want)
Earings (up to two of the same kind)
Wrist wear (up to two of the same kind)
Neck wear (up to two of the same kind)
 
Even with the above exemptions you may not store more than 50 items in one
"save" room.  While the mud does not enforce this restriction, random
inspections from immortals are not uncommon.  Violation of this hoarding
policy will result in immediate purge of the offending room.
 
You may build a home in Residential Helopolis.  Prime locations (river
front, good views, etc...) are reserved for larger houses (six rooms or
more). Once you choose a location you're stuck with it unless you choose to
buy a new room for your home, at which time you may move to another
location within the same town.  You cannot move your house to another zone,
though you may own more than one house.

Groups of ten or more players wishing to start a condo association may get
together and purchase a tract of land for 100k coins plus 250k per unit.
Condos have a group fountain and no internal running water.  Cats are
allowed as pets but you cannot add on.  Units are not relocatable but can
be traded in for 75% of their value when a player is ready to "move up".

If you would like to buy a house, find the realtor's office in the 
residential area that interests you.  If there is no realtor's office 
in the area, then you probably cannot buy a house there.  The realtor's 
office contains all the policies, pricing, and answers to questions about 
houses.

See Also: HOUSE
#0
HUMAN

Humans are the most numerous of the PhoenixMUD races.  They exist in 
almost all terrains and climates, and have an amazing knack for 
adaptation.  There's a good deal of variation between regions with 
humans, including skin color, eye and hair color, build, and customs, but 
for all intents and purposes they're similar enough to be considered 
identical.

Humans are the standard that all other races are compared against, and as 
such, they're best known for their mediocrity.  Humans aren't 
particularly fast, nor are they amazingly strong.  However, their 
generalization often gives them advantages over more specialized races, 
like minotaurs and elves.  Humans are usually well-suited for any 
profession, and they suffer no obvious disadvantages.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
IMPLEMENTOR IMP HIMP

Implementors are the most powerful beings in existence.  The Head-
Implementors are the founders of the realms and the supreme gods.
An implementor's word is law.  It would be a wise idea not to 
disobey one of these powerful dieties.
#0
INN INNS

Inns are places where the weary traveler may rest while the innkeeper looks
after his equipment and treasure.

Specifically, the game will save your equipment while you are logged out.
There is a daily cost for renting, based on your equipment.  Each item has
a per diem charge.  The per diem charge is calculated to the nearest second,
however -- if you rent at the rate of 2400 coins per day, and you come back
39 hours later, you will be charged 3900 coins.

Rent files are deleted if you run out of money to pay the per diem charges
or after 30 days.  

See also: OFFER RENT CAMP
#0
KENSAI

Translated to mean "Sword Saint", they are the true embodiment of the
fighting spirit, they are considered to be the greatest combatants in the
world.  They undergo extremely tough mental conditioning and training,
being hand picked from the elite potential of other professions.  Whilst
they may fight for a cause of their own choice, one thing remains steady, a
kensai's honor, which is valued above nearly all else stops them from
learning magic for an offensive matter, relying solely on their superior
skills and tactics in close quarter combat.

See also: CLASSES REMORT
#0
LIGHT FUEL

Most, if not all, non-magical light sources may be refilled, if you have the
proper type of fuel.  The amount of fuel that they hold, and the efficiency of
that fuel depends on the type.  The three types of fuels in use on Phoenix are
coal, oil, and fat.  You cannot use coal in an oil lamp for example.  To 
refill a light, you would use the command REFUEL.

See Also: REFUEL
#0
LINK

A lot of players have problems with their links to the game.  This can be
very hazardous to your health in the middle of a battle, of course.

 I. If your link is broken, you will automatically attempt to flee each
    time someone attempts to hit you.  If you manage to flee, then you
    will be saved by divine forces, and put in a safe place until you
    reconnect to the game.

II. If your link jams (freezes), you have a problem.  The only solution we
    can give is that you break your link as quickly as possible.  By
    breaking the link you can hope that the above procedure is initiated.

    Do not try to get out of a fight by breaking link.  You will be not
    reimbursed for any experience or equipment if you purposefully
    cut your link.
#0
MAGIC-USER WIZARD MAGE

Magic users are mighty magicians, having spent long years learning the
secrets of magic.  They know many spells, offensive and defensive, but
lack the curative magics of clerics.  Their studies often leave them weak
and frail, so a magic user is wise to take along a stronger companion for
protection.  They are also lacking in combat skills, putting them at a
disadvantage when it comes to swinging swords, but their magic more than
makes up for this.

See also: CLASSES
#0
MINOTAUR

Minotaurs are a race of large humanoids, resembling powerful men with the 
heads of bulls.  They usually stand about seven feet tall, weighing from 
three hundred to four hundred pounds, all of which is muscle.  
They are a race of warriors and raiders, and pride themselves 
on their strength and combat abilities.  Humans fear these 
beasts like the plague -- while brutal, powerful and tough, like ogres 
and trolls, they have brains to match their prowess, making them 
dangerous indeed.

Being built as they are, minotaurs are much stronger than humans.  They 
are very resistant to wounds, heal quickly, and take a long time to 
tire.  Their big bodies are a little slower than humans, but not by 
much.  While just as intelligent as the average human, they are not a 
race known for their wisdom, which is usually poor.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
MOOD

Mobs have various moods in PhoenixMUD 4.0.  What does this mean for you,
the player?  It means several things.  Mobs may react to how you treat them
by doing things back to you.  Citizens of towns may call for the guards to
assist them.  Guards may call on other guards for assistance.  If a mob
is pissed off enough and you set them over the top they may just outright
attack you.  The moral of the story is to be careful with what you do around
mobs in the game.
#0
MONK

Monks are a group of mystical warrior-priests, excelling in hand-to-hand
combat.  If there is a way to injure an opponent using one's body, monks
know how to do it. Part of their training includes the use of some
clerical magic, but this is mainly confined to curative and protective
spells.  Still, monks are more than capable in combat, and coupled with
their magic, they're a force to be reckoned with. Monks are also the only
known class who can cure the dreaded plague.

See Also: CLASSES
#0
MOVE HOMETOWN

Initially, everyone's hometown is set to Heliopolis, home of the Temple of
the Phoenix.  This means that when you recall, use a scroll of recall, or
the Word of Recall spell, you are sent to the Temple of the Phoenix in
Heliopolis.

Some of the other cities in the realm are also hometowns.  To change your
hometown, find a receptionist in your desired city and look at her.  If her
description includes instructions on how to change hometowns, you can
follow those instructions and change your hometown, thus changing the
location of the place you are sent when you recall.
#0
NAME

Name Policy
 
In the recent effort to promote a medieval, fantasy atmosphere throughout the
realms of Phoenix Mud, a new name policy regarding character creation has
been established.  Under this new policy, the following guidelines must be
adhered to when choosing a new name:
 
[1] Names must NOT be composed of common words, including common nouns, verbs,
    propositions, or any combination thereof.
    For example:  EvilMage, Flower, Backstab, DarkOne
 
[2] No obscene, insulting, threatening or degrading names will be tolerated.
    For example: (No examples needed)
 
[3] No modern trademarks, cartoon characters, product names, etc. are allowed.
    For example:  Beavis, Bacardi, Seinfeld
 
[4] No incoherent collection of random letters is allowed.
    For example:  Wqzlks, Xeyplw
 
[5] No names that imply rank or privilage.
    For example: King, Lady
 
[6] No animal names.
    For example: Cougar, Puma, Snake, etc.

[7] No Capital letters in the name other then the first letter
    For example: STUDBOY
    Exception: Names like McCleod.
 
[8] _Do_ choose a medieval type of name.  If you have any questions regarding
    the acceptability of a certain name, feel free to ask a God for input
    and guidance.

[9] A god has final say on any name choice.  Ignoring or arguing with the god who
    is trying to enforce this policy is a quick way of getting deleted.
#0
NECROMANCER

The darkest form of magic wielder known to exist, their coersion with
demons and the undead is well documented and highly feared.   Only the most
talented exponents of this art are chosen to study in the guilds that the
Necromancer calls home.  Furthermore, the Necromancer becomes more and more
twisted the more powerful they become and the further they delve into their
dark art.  However unlike other spellcasters, the needs of close quarter
combat hasn't been lost upon them, and necromancers are able to use almost
all manner of weapons in combat.

See also: CLASSES REMORT
#0
OBJECT OBJECTS

Objects on PhoenixMUD can and will take damage and eventually will get
destroyed.  Each object is made of a specfic material type.  This material
type affects how strong the object is and how much damage it can take. 
As items take damage they will be readily identified by looking at your
inventory or equipment lists, or using the Identify spell on the object.
Objects can be repaired, but objects which are repaired often will not
have the strength and resistance of brand new objects.
#0
PALADIN

Paladins are the militant arm of the holy orders of clerics, taking faith
to the enemy with a sword.  They're very capable fighters, having a
variety of combat skills and techniques, and their religious studies and
faith give them the ability to use some clerical magics.  Although not
quite as effective a fighter as a warrior, or as able with spells as a
cleric, the combination of the two makes them effective in all situations.
Paladins will lose their god-given powers if they stray from the path of
good and righteousness.

See Also: CLASSES
#0
RACES

There are currently twelve races available for mortals on PhoenixMUD.
Upon remorting twice, four additional races are available.  These races
will be detailed in a seperate help file.   Class selection is based
directly on race selection.  Some races are better at certain professions
than other races.  Some professions are not available at all to certain
races.
 
The twelve initial races include: 
     Human     - Normal in all abilities. 
     Elf       - Highly intelligent, but not very strong. 
     Half-Elf  - Stronger than elves, not as intelligent. 
     Dark-Elf  - Evil. Harmed by sunlight. NOT FOR BEGINNERS. 
     Dwarf     - Stout and strong, but not very intelligent. 
     Halfling  - Agile and quick, but not very strong. 
     Sprite    - Excellent intelligence, little strength. 
     Minotaur  - Strong, fairly intelligent, less agile. 
     Avian     - Agile, Intelligent, little strength. 
     Half-Ogre - Strong and dumb brutes. 
     Half-Orc  - Stronger than human, not as intelligent.
       

See also: HUMAN ELF H_ELF DARK_ELF DWARF HALFLING SPRITE MINOTAUR
          AVIAN H_OGRE H_ORC RACESKILLS
#0
RANGED

PhoenixMUD allows you to fight monsters from afar, using ranged weapons.  There
are several kinds of ranged weapons available, including throwable items, bows,
crossbows, slings, and grenades.  You must have the proper kinds of ammunition
for each weapon before you can use it.  Slings use rocks, bows use arrows, and
crossbows use bolts.  These weapons have a limited range of one to three rooms.
Arrows may be destroyed or lost when they are used.  Monsters will remember who
shot them and may shoot back or come after you.

See Also: PULL THROW SHOOT
#0
RANGER

Rangers are defenders of nature, having learned its ways and appreciating
its beauty.  Aside from being very capable in combat, they know a variety
of skills pertaining to the outdoors.  They also have some knowledge of
magic, generally spells concerning animals and nature.  This, combined
with their skill at tracking their quarry, makes them a welcome addition
to any party and well-rounded adventurers in their own right.

See Also: CLASSES
#0
SHADOW_RACE

By the little history that is known of their kind, they are by their own
records the oldest race known to exist, having existed before the gods saw
fit to allow light onto this realm.  An enigmatic and malevolent kind, in
the limited meetings with their kind they are reportedly without any
visible physical form, projecting only a shadow of a being.  They do
however have extreme awareness, noticing even the most minor thing and
disappearing into nothingness.  From reports from the other elder races,
they do have a physical form, but they mastered the art of hiding their
true form from all onlookers long before the birth of the younger races.

See also: RACES REMORT
#0
SHOP_COMMANDS
Commands
--------
* &Wshop list&n
  If you're standing in a shop, see everything that's for sale.
* &Wshop list ring&n
  See all objects for sale with "ring" in their name.
* &Wshop buy #4&n
  Buy item #4 from the shop (must have enough gold on hand).
 
Owner only
----------
* &Wshop add ring 10000&n
  Add the ring from your inventory for sale for 10K coins.
* &Wshop remove #5&n
  Removes item #5 from the shop and back into your inventory.
 
#0
SPRITE

Sprites are tiny faerie creatures, usually keeping within the confines of 
their forests.  All are very curious, but it's only the incredibly 
curious (and incredibly foolhardy) ones that venture out into the rest of 
the world.  Sprites are not inherently magical as most people believe; 
it's just that they are well suited to casting spells, and that's often 
the profession they choose.

Sprites are extremely wise and intelligent, making them well-suited to 
using magic.  Their small size also makes them very quick.  The downside 
of their size is their strength, which is very low, and their resistance 
to damage.  (Or lack of it.)

Sprite have inherent powers of levitation and invisibility.  A sprite
must exercise its willpower to become visible to other creatures.

See also: RACES RACESKILLS
#0
THIEF

Thieves are rogues, bandits, and just about anyone else willing to make
some money at someone else's misfortune.  They have some knowledge of
combat, making them decent at it, if not effective, and excel at sneaking
around, hiding, and generally not being noticed.  Their training does not
include magic, however, and thieves are precluded from using spells.
Their skills make them good at scouting and getting past obstacles,
though, and even though they're frowned upon in most areas, they're
generally welcome in an adventuring party.
 
By the way...don't even THINK about stealing from another player.  The
authorities only allow for so much before they take action, and they have
an uncanny knack for spotting pstealers...

See also: CLASSES
#0
TITAN

Whilst not as large as the name may suggest, they've come to like the title
the humans have given them.  Whilst in appearance they look like humans,
their height is much like the half-ogre, whilst their muscle size is
comparable to a similar sized Demon.  They are an ancient and extremely
powerful race. On a whole, they keep to themselves, but those that have
made contact with the younger races speak of their elders reaching ages the
range into millenia.  Extremely strong and resistant to physical damage,
having been seen turning their skin to a rocklike substance at a whim,
however they don't seem to lack the agility or intellect that other races
of a similar size suffer, although even with their high intellect, very few
that have been come across dabble intensively within the realms of magic.

See also: RACES REMORT
#0
WARRIOR

Warriors are the soldiers and fighters of the world, enduring harsh
training and rigorous discipline to become the powerful combatants.
They train extensively in all aspects of fighting, from defensive
techniques to unarmed combat, making them the masters of their art.
Warrior training does not include magic use of any kind, however, and
warriors are unable to use spells.

See Also: CLASSES
#0
MOBPROGS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Mobile Programs are better known as mobprogs.  Mobprogs are used to
give a mobile life, and therefore give the entire world a bit more
than your standard hack and slash MUD.  While mobprogs are not an
essential part of a mobile they do make the game a whole lot more
interesting, and exciting.  Before creating a mobprog, obviously you
need to create a mob.  If you are unsure how to create a mob, consult
your another immortal or read help medit.
 
There are three very simple steps to creating a mobprog.  The first is
to select your type.  The second is to select your argument and the
third is to select the commands which are acted out by the mob.  Think
of a mobprog as though it were a gun.  The argument is the finger that
pulls the trigger (the type), and the trigger forces the command out
of the barrel of the gun.  That in a nutshell is how a mobprog works.
To learn about what types there are, and how they work referense HELP
TYPES.  To learn about what kind of arguments are required for certain
types read HELP ARGUMENTS.  To learn about how commands are inputted
read HELP MOBCOMMANDS.


MOBPROG TYPES  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 0) INFILE
        An INFILE program is basically a program that does nothing.  It is
        used only as a "place holder" for a mobile program.
 
 1) ACT
        An ACT program is used to allow the mobile to perform a certain
        action when an event triggers
 
 2) SPEECH
        A SPEECH program allows a builder to inbue the mobile with the
        ability to respond to a phrase or word said by a player or other         
        mobile.
 
 3) RAND
        A RAND program gives the MOB the ability to randomly perform an
        event, from saying something to scratching itself.
 
 4) FIGHT        
        A FIGHT program is used by the builder to give the mob a certain
        personality in a fight.  This can be used to give the mobile certain
        actions or phrases they will do during a fight.
 
 5) DEATH
        A DEATH program can be utalized to give the mobile a "death scene."
        With this program a builder can make the mobile do anything from 
        say its last words or perform some action right before it dies.
 
 6) HITPRCNT
        A HITPRCNT program is used to give a mobile the ability to speak at
        a certain point in a fight, specifically when x% amount of their 
        hitpoints have been reduced.
 
 7) ENTRY
        The ENTRY program gives a mobile a phrase or action that they will 
        give whenever they enter a room.
 
 8) GREET
        The GREET program is used to allow a mobile to greet any visible 
        person who enters the room with a phrase or action.
 
 9) ALL_GREET
        A ALL_GREET program is exactly like GREET except that it allows the 
        mobile to greet even hidden or sneaking players or other mobiles.
 
10) GIVE
        The GIVE program is set by builders to give the mobile the ability 
        to respond in any method they choose when given an object (best 
        used on quests).
 
11) BRIBE
        BRIBE is a mobile program that gives a mobile a price.  When the 
        mobile is paid a certain amount of money it will perform the command.
 
ARGUMENTS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
An argument is what sets off the trigger (or type) of the mobile
program.  This argument is sometimes a number (representing a percent
of monotary figure) or a phrase that is read in a social/emote of
message by a player.  The following is a list of how the argument
would be worded for each TYPE of mobprog.
 
 1) INFILE
        An INFILE program is basically a program that does nothing.  It is
        used only as a "place holder" for a mobile program and thefore has 
        no need for an argument.
 
 2) ACT
        An ACT argument would consist of what event triggers the actions 
        on the part of the mobile.  In this case the argument would be 
        structured as follows - p pokes you in the ribs.  The p represents 
       that it is in fact a phrase and the period at the end signifies that 
        it must appear exactly as shown.  The period is to prevent occurances 
        such as THE being the argument as words such as there or then
        triggering it.
 
 3) SPEECH
        A SPEECH argument would be what phrase said by a player triggers 
        the set actions of the mobile.  The argument for a SPEECH program 
        would be similar to the following - p hello.  The p signifies that 
        it is a phrase and the period shows that hello must appear exactly 
        as it has been typed.
 
 4) FIGHT
        The argument of a FIGHT program would be a number, which represents 
        a percent.  If the builder wanted a mobile to do an action 5% of the 
        time the argument would be inputted as simply 5.
 
 5) DEATH
        The DEATh program's argument is also a percent.
 
 6) HITPRCNT
        The HITPRCNT program also has a percent for an argument.
 
 7) ENTRY
        The ENTRY program also uses a percent as an argument.
 
 8) GREET
        The GREET program is also an argument of percents.  The size of this
        number depends on the consistancy with which the builder wishes the 
        mobile to greet incoming players or other mobiles.
 
 9) GREET_ALL
        See GREET.
 
10) GIVE
        The argument for GIVE is the name of the object the builder wishes
        the mobile to respond to.  If the objects name were bottle, one would
        simply type bottle into the argument field.
 
11) BRIBE
        The BRIBE program required an argument of an amount of money.  The 
        amount of money is takes to BRIBE the mobile into performing the 
        action defined in commands.  If the price were 100 gold piece one 
        would simply type 100 into the argument section.
 
MOB PROG COMMANDS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

The COMMANDS section of the mobile program works exactly like the text
editor for describing a room or object.  A builder simply types in the
action he or she wishes the mobile to perform, saves it and exits.
But, always when making a command for a mobile in a mobprog one must
be sure and inclue the word BREAK on a new line of the program,
otherwise the program will loop, and invariably crash the MUD.  An
example of a command section of a mobprog would resemble the
following.
 
>if SEX == 2
>kiss $n
>BREAK
 
This command tells the mobile that if the person who set off the
trigger is a female then the mobile should kiss her.  The $n
represents the name of the person who set the trigger off so the
mobile doesn't get the wrong person.  The following is a list of
variables similar to $n.
 
$i      the first of the names of the mobile itself.
$I      the short description of the mobile itself.
$n      the name of whomever caused the trigger to happen.
$N      the name and title of whomever caused the trigger to happen.
$t      the name of a secondary character target (i.e A smiles at B)
$T      the short description, or name and title of target (NPC vs PC)
$r      the name of a random char in the room with the mobile (never == $i)
$R      the short description, or name and title of the random char
 
$j      he,she,it based on sex of $i.
$e      he,she,it based on sex of $n.
$E      he,she,it based on sex of $t.
$J      he,she,it based on sex of $r.
 
$k      him,her,it based on sex of $i.
$m      him,her,it based on sex of $n.
$M      him,her,it based on sex of $t.
$K      him,her,it based on sex of $r.
 
$l      his,hers,its based on sex of $i.
$s      his,hers,its based on sex of $n.
$S      his,hers,its based on sex of $t.
$L      his,hers,its based on sex of $r.
 
$o      the first of the names of the primary object (i.e A drops B)
$O      the short description of the primary object
$p      the first of the names of the secondary object (i.e A puts B in C)
$P      the short description of the secondary object
 
$a      a,an based on first character of $o
$A      a,an based on first character of $p
