STANDARDS
Area Size

In order to qualify for an immortal or enhanced mortal on the playing
port a builder must create a zone of no fewer than 50 rooms. Under
special circumstances several smaller zones will be accepted in lieu
of a single larger zone.

Room Length

All rooms must consist of at least three lines which describe the
room, not the exits from the room. Every room has something special,
even if what is special is that it is totally unremarkable. Rooms
should not be more than 8-10 lines in length--more than that and they
get incredibly boring for everyone involved.

Description Content

Don't tell the character what he feels emotionally. To say 'You are
scared' is unacceptable. To say 'The dark and foggy atmosphere is
chilling and would scare even the most stalwart of adventurers' is
acceptable. Don't EVER tell a character what he thinks or wonders. To
say 'You wonder what is behind that door' is unacceptable. To say 'The
strange markings and huge locks on the outside of the door must be
keeping something valuable...or dangerous...within' is
acceptable. Don't ever talk about a humanoid mob masturbating,
urinating, or defecating... no one wants to hear about it. Including
any of these in your zone will cause it to be rejected until the
offending material is removed. When choosing your vocabulary don't use
'playground' words that your grammar school teachers would have
forbidden in their classroom. There is really no reason to use
them. Specifically, including the words 'shit, fuck, asshole, and cum'
in your descriptions will get your zone rejected.

Typos/Spelling/Grammar

We know everyone can't be a super-speller/grammatician . But everyone
can at least remove typos from their room descriptions. Please try to
do so.  Spelling and grammar will be fixed by a team leader or other
zone- reviewer at the time of review. However, if your grammar sucks
to a point where the reviewer cannot understand what you are trying to
say your zone will be rejected. You are expected to READ AND LEARN the
items in the Language Tips section of the webpage--visit it every once
in a while to see if there are additions, bookmark it!


Uniqueness

We expect that all builders will be original zones unless they are
otherwise authorized. You can take your ideas from roleplaying game
modules and even your descriptions, but you CANNOT steal areas from
another mud that YOU did not build. Any builder found to have stolen
areas from another mud will be deleted and will not be eligible for
another builder EVER.


Repeat Room Descriptions

You may use the same description for a room only once per 33 rooms. So
if you have a 33 room zone every room description MUST be different.
If you have a 34 room zone you may have two rooms with the same
description, in a 67 room zone you may have three, and so on. An
exception to this is made in the case of mazes but they must be
authorized by your Team Leader beforehand.


Need for an Area

Please make sure that your zone is needed before commencing work on
it.  You can do this by authorizing your zone idea with your Team
Leader. If there are 40 newbie zones in the world then we really don't
need yet another. If there are 3 dwarf towns then we don't need
another--please use your brain and look at what other zones there are
in the world before deciding on one. If you need help thinking of
something just talk to other builders on any of the teams.


Area Theme

Your area must have a purpose besides providing a place for people to
kill random monsters and get experience. It has to fit into the world
somehow.  PhoenixMUD has a medieval/fantasy theme and all areas should
fit into it.  For a really well-written zone a rift *can* be
created--but we prefer medieval/fantasy.

Mapability

It should be possible to map your area on paper with a minimal
confusion.  This doesn't mean don't make it complex, just make it
possible for an immortal to map it easily using vnums and for a
careful mortal to map it.  If Cymynedd can't map your zone using vnums
you will be asked to mail her (via paper mail or if you have a scanner
via email) a *neatly* drawn map which includes vnums and room names.

Implementation into the World

YOU are responsible for figuring out how to link your zone to the rest
of the world unless your Team Leader tells you differently. This means
you have to pick out a couple different places you think your area
would fit and write the appropriate connection rooms. 


Room Names

Unless the person who reviews your zone volunteers to do it for you,
you must capitalize your room names properly yourself. This is a major
hassle to do after the fact so you might as well do it right when
building. The first letter of ANY room name is capitalized, no matter
what. Then capitalize the first letter of each of the following words
unless they are unimportant like "of" "a" "the". Do not tell the
person exactly what room they are in unless it is apparent from the
description. Rather than say "The King Ogre's Bedroom" say "A Royal
Bedroom" or "The Royal Bedroom".

Mob/Object Descriptions

All mobs and objects should have a short and long descriptions (except
for special objects like bookcases which are mentioned in the room
description--those don't need long descriptions) as well as detailed/
extra descriptions. A zone full of objects with no extra descriptions
is an incredible yawner. Make the descriptions interesting/amusing or
include clues in them in order to encourage players to look at them.

Exit Descriptions

Include exit descriptions for all rooms. This is ESPECIALLY important
for death traps but still very important for the rest of the rooms.


Room Extra Descriptions

There should be a MINIMUM of one *useful* extra description per five 
rooms. Individual Team Leaders may increase this minimum.

Mob/Object Supply

There should be a MAXIMUM of one *loaded* mob per two rooms. So if you
write a ten room zone and want 5 mobs in one room then none of the
other rooms can have mobs, even if all five of those mobs are the same
thing--there will be only ONE mob in your .mob file. There should be a
MINIMUM of one mob per five rooms--fewer than this and the zone gets
boring. The same maximums and minimums go for individual objects but
you can load as many or as few as you like. If you have 5 ogre
warriors feel free to equip them all with chainmail and swords.

Mob/Object Stats

Stats on mobs are automatically generated so you don't need to bother
with HP/hitroll/damroll/AC, gold, and experience. Stats on objects
should be consistent with the rest of the world--any ridiculous stats
will be changed. All personal items or items which do not fit into the
theme of the world will be removed. Immortals are allowed one personal
item per zone created but the items MUST be in zone 12 and cannot have
any stats and must be level limited to 104. The highest damage allowed
for any normal weapon is an average 20. This INCLUDES plusses to
damroll. Items like this should load ONLY on level 100 and over
mobs. If you want to create a special weapon with a higher average you
must obtain permission from Aleksandr AND Cymynedd or your item will
be modified during zone review.

Stay In Zone Flags

All mobs must be flagged with the stay zone flag to keep them from
wandering away.
#0
ADDPOINT

Usage: addpoint <player>

This command adds a single quest point to the specified player.  Quest
points are given out for the successful completion of quests and there
are usage guidelines.  Check the immortal handbook for more information.

See Also: DELPOINT
#104
ADDXNAME

Usage addxname [name]

The addxname command allows a god to view the xnames file and also to
add to it on the fly.  For those not familiar, the xnames file is a
text file of names that are not allowed for character names.  There
are numerous lewd words listed, as well as other silliness, such as
studboy.  After asking someone to change a ridiculous name, it would
probably be a wise idea to add that name to the xnames file.  Typing
addxname without an argument simply lists the file.

See Also: NAME
#104
ADJUSTMOB ADJUSTZONE ADJUSTSHOP ADJUSTROOM ADJUSTOBJ

Usage: adjust(mob|obj|zone|shop|room)

These are EXTREMELY powerful commands and should not be used without 
consulting Masque.  These commands cause all files of the particular
type to be written to disk with a global change made.  In order to use
the commands code must be written first.  As an example, adjustmob was
used to make a global change to all mobs to fix their position values.
These commands can cause a LOT of damage if used improperly.
#125
ADVANCE

Usage: advance <victim> <level>

ADVANCE moves a player to a new level.  If used to promote a player to an
immortal level, and if you have the autowiz program installed, the wizlist and
immlist will automatically be recreated.

Example:

  > advance rasmussen 34

See also: SET
#104
WIZAT "AT"

Usage: at <room VNUM | item | mob | player name> <command> <arguments>

AT can be used to perform an action in a location other than where you are
standing.  The first argument can be a virtual room number or the name of a
character or object.  The remainder of the line is passed to the command
interpreter.

Examples:

  > at 3001 look
  > at fido kill fido
  > at 3.corpse get all corpse
  > at catje wave catje

See also: GOTO
#104
ATTACH   

Usage:  attach <mtr | otr | wtr> <vnum> <target> <location>

This command adds a trigger to the mob, object, or room specified. 
Location indicates which position the new trigger should be in. The
location parameter is optional, with the default being the last
position.

See also:  DETACH
#104
BAN UNBAN

Usage: ban [<all | new | select> <site>]
       unban <site>

These commands prevent anyone from a site with a hostname containing the
site substring from logging in to the game.  You may ban a site to ALL, NEW
or SELECT players.  Banning a site to NEW players prevents any new players
from registering.  Banning a site to ALL players disallows ANY connections
from that site.  Banning a site SELECTively allows only players with site-ok
flags to log in from that site.  Ban with no argument returns a list of
currently banned sites.

Unban removes the ban.

Examples:

  > ban all whitehouse.gov
  > unban ai.mit.edu

See also: WIZLOCK IDENT
#104
BCONTROL

Usage: bcontrol <open/close> <low level> <high level>
       bcontrol lock

This command controls the opening and closing of the battle field.  A god
must open the field for players to fight.  By following the syntax above,
you can open the arena to selected levels, determined by the <high> and
<low> arguments to the command.

> bcontrol open 30 50

This will open the battlefield for levels 30 to 50.

> bcontrol close

This will close the battlefield.

> bcontrol lock

This will prevent new contestants from joining the battlefield.

See Also: BATTLE BWHO BSAY
#104
BECOME

Usage: become <playername>

This command is used to switch into a linkloaded or linkdead player
character. It functions the same way as SWITCH does. Abuse of this command
will subject the abusing god to demotion and possibly deletion.

See Also: SWITCH RETURN LINKLOAD
#104
"BID STOP"

Usage: bid stop

The BID STOP command allows an immortal to stop an auction already in progress.
The immortal will receive the item that had been auctioned and no bidders
will lose their money.

See Also: AUCTION BID
#104
DATE

Shows the current real time. (Not a social)
#104
DC

Usage: dc <descriptor number>

DC (DisConnect) is used to disconnect a socket.  If used on a playing socket,
the player will lose his/her link, but, unlike PURGE, will not extract the
player or dump the player's inventory on the ground.

DC is also useful for disconnecting people who have accidentally left
themselves at the main menu, or connections sitting in the "Get name" state.

See also: USERS
#104
DELAY PS QKILL
 
Syntax: delay <time> <target> <command to execute>
	ps
	qkill <job number>
 
There is a new queue system on Phoenix MUD.  What this means is that you
can enter commands into the queue for execution at a later time.  To view
what is currently in the queue, type PS.  To enter a command into the
queue, the DELAY command is used.  You can enter NEARLY ANY TYPE of
command to be executed.  The <time to delay> argument is the time in real
seconds.  To kill a job in the queue, use PS to find the job number, and
then use QKILL and the job number to kill it.
 
Example:
 
>delay 20 swordmaster gos This is a test of the Queue
--This will cause the first instance of 'swordmaster' to gossip, "This is a
  test of the Queue" 20 seconds from now.
>ps
--Find your job in the queue list. Note the job number.
>qkill 5
--Kill job 5 in the queue.
 
Due to the nature of this command, any abuse of the command queue will
NOT be tolerated, and will result in the removal of the command
privelages at the least, and quite possibly deletion.
 
See Also: FORCE
#104
DELPOINT

Usage: delpoint <player>

This command removes a single quest point from a player.

See Also: ADDPOINT
#104
DETACH

Syntax:  detach [mob | object | room] <target> <num | all>

This command removes a trigger from the mob, object, or room specified. 
"All" will remove all  the triggers. If a number is used instead, the
trigger in that position on the script  will be removed. Number may also
be a vnum.

See also:  ATTACH
#104
DISTRIBUTE

Usage: distribute { obj | mob } <v_number> <start> <end> <amount>

This command is used to distribute a large number of like mobs and objects
throughout a series of rooms.  This would mainly be used for quest purposes.
In the above syntax, the variables mean the following:

<v_number>--The virtual number of the mob or object you wish to distribute.
<start><end>--The starting and ending vnums of the rooms you wish to 
	distribute in.
<amount>--The number of mobs/objs you wish to distribute.

Example: distribute mob 3062 3001 3014 20

The previous example will distribute 20 mobs of type 3062 (fido) through
rooms 3001 to 3014.
#104
ECHO GECHO QECHO SEND

Usage: echo <message>
       gecho <message>
       send <victim> <message>
       qecho <message>

ECHO displays a string to everyone in your room.
GECHO displays the string to everyone in the game.
QECHO displays the string to everyone with the QUEST flag.
SEND displays the string to a particular person.

Examples:

  > echo Hi, everyone in the room.
  > gecho Hi, everyone in the game, isn't this annoying?
  > send rasmussen You are being watched.
#104
FORCE

Usage: force <victim | all> <command> <arguments>

FORCE forces a character, either monster or player, to perform a certain
action.  FORCE ALL forces everyone in the game; FORCE ROOM forces everyone in
your room.

Example:

  > force fido drop meat
  > force all save
  > force room look fido

If you are invisible to the one being forced, the force will be performed
silently.
#104
FREEZE THAW

Usage: freeze <victim>
       thaw <victim>

FREEZE, originally invented here at CircleMUD (we think), is used to prevent
a player from playing.  If frozen, the game will ignore all commands entered
by the player, including QUIT, until the player is unfrozen with THAW.

Obviously, this command should only be used in extreme disciplinary
circumstances.

See also: SET -> FROZEN
#104
GEDIT

Usage:

> gedit <virtual guild num>         
Edit/create guild with the virtual number specified

> gedit save <zone>
Save all the guilds in zone to disk

> gedit save all
Save all guild files which have been edited to disk.

Gedit is the guild editor portion of OLC.  Gedit allows you to edit
all aspects of guilds (training locations) in the game.  You will only be
allowed to edit/create guilds in the zone for which you have permissions.
Please consult your builder's handbook for explanations of individual
fields and flags.

See Also: OASIS OLC MEDIT OEDIT REDIT SEDIT ZEDIT EDITOR
#104
GODLOG

Usage: godlog <what you want inserted into the log>

The godlog command simply inserts a string of text into the syslogs.  It is
preceded with the (GC) characters to denote that it is a god command.  It
may be a good idea to insert a comment into the logs when you do something
that may be construed as breaking the rules.  The most recent contents in the
syslogs can be viewed with the VSYSLOG command.

For Example
> give 10000 coins kinthor
> godlog that was a prize for a difficult quest that kinthor won.

See Also: VSYSLOG
#104
GOTO TRANS TRANSFER WIZTELEPORT TELEPORT

Usage: goto <location>
       trans <victim>
       teleport <victim> <location>

These commands are used to transports yourself and others to different rooms.
GOTO allows you to go to other rooms; TRANS transports a person from wherever
they are to the room YOU are in; TELEPORT transports your victim to some other
location.

For both GOTO and TELEPORT, the location specifier can be either a virtual
room number, or the name of a character or object.

Examples:

  > goto 3001
  > goto rasmussen
  > trans fido
  > teleport fido 3001
  > teleport fido 2.fido

See also: POOFIN, POOFOUT, SET -> ROOM, WIZAT
#104
HANDBOOK

Usage: handbook

Guidelines for having an Immortal character -- you should read it.
#0
HCONTROL

Usage: hcontrol build <house vnum> <exit dir> <player name>
       hcontrol destroy <house vnum>
       hcontrol pay <house vnum>
       hcontrol show

House control controls the addition, removal, and payments of houses.
Hcontrol show will show all the houses currently defined in the game
along with their last 'rent' payment date.

See also: HOUSE, HOUSES
#104
HELPCHECK

Usage: helpcheck

Helpcheck displays a list of all commands in the mud which do not have
a corresponding help entry.  This is useful for gods who write help
entries to find out which commands do not have entries.
#104
HOLYLIGHT

Usage: holylight

A toggle which allows you to see:
   Invisible people and objects, even if you don't have 'detect invisibility'
   Hidden people, even if you don't have 'sense life'
   Dark rooms, even if you don't have a light

It is expected most immortals will keep HOLYLIGHT on most of the time;
it exists so that you have the ability to see what mortals see for
debugging purposes.
#104
HOME

Usage: home <playername>

Home sends you back to your start room, usually 1204 for gods.  Specifying a target 
with the home command sends that person back to their starting room.  It is similar
to the RECALL command.

See Also: RECALL
#104
IDENT

Usage: ident

This will toggle the ident capability on or off.  Ident allows incoming
usernames to be resolved if the remote server is running the ident daemon.
Ident is always on by default.  Change in config.c to turn it off by
default.  

It takes up to 30 seconds to query for the username of the incoming
connection.  To change this, change IDENT_TIMEOUT in ident.c

UPDATE: As of 8/15/2001 this command is OFF by default and
should be left that way.

See Also: BAN
#104
IDLE

Usage: idle < zone | '*' | '.' >

The idle command will set the time for a zone equal to its lifespan, which
will have the effect of causing the zone to idle out of memory after a
minute.  This is a useful technique for builders, as zreseting a zone
after it has idled will cause any SYSERRS for that zone to be displayed.
Idle * will cause the entire world to be idled out.  Idle . will idle out
the zone you are currently in.

See Also: ZRESET
#104
IMOTD

Usage: imotd

Displays the Immortal message of the day.  This file usually contains
new information on commands recently added, new features and other
important information that immortals should be aware of.

See also: MOTD
#104
WIZINVIS

Usage: invis [level]

INVIS sets your invisibility level.  With no argument, invis toggles between
making you fully visible and fully invisible.  If you specify a level, only
people at and above the level you specify will be able to see you.

Examples:

  > invis
  > invis 31
#104
LAST

Usage: last <player_name>

For checking the last time a person logged on, their sitename, and their
ID number.

Example: 

  > last ras
  [  24] [14 Cl] Ras          : somesite.edu : Tue May 16 22:07:09 1995

See also: STAT
#104
LINKLOAD

Usage: linkload <playername>

This command allows you to load a player from the playerfile while they are
not present.  Usually used in conjunction with the BECOME command. 

See Also: RETURN BECOME SWITCH
#104
LOAD

Usage: load <mob | obj> <virtual number>

LOAD is used to create mobiles and objects.  The first argument specifies if
you are trying to load a mobile or an object; the second is the virtual
number.

Example:

  > load obj 3099
  You create a bulletin board.

  > load mob 3005
  You create the receptionist.

See also: VNUM
#104
MEDIT

Usage:

> medit <virtual mob num>         
Edit/create mobile with the virtual number specified

> medit save <zone>
Save all the mobiles in zone to disk

> medit save all
Save all mob files which have been edited to disk.

Medit is the mob editor portion of OLC.  Medit allows you to edit
all aspects of mobiles (monsters) in the game.  You will only be
allowed to edit/create mobs in the zone for which you have permissions.
Please consult your builder's handbook for explanations of individual
fields and flags.

See Also: OASIS OLC GEDIT OEDIT REDIT SEDIT ZEDIT EDITOR
#104
MUTE

Usage: mute <victim>

MUTE is used to shut a player up.  When muted, a player will not be able to
use most communication channels until he/she is unmuted.

See also: FLAGS
#104
NOHASSLE

Usage: nohassle

Toggles a flag to prevent aggressive monsters from attacking.
#104
NOTITLE

Usage: notitle <vicitim>

Prevent players from being able to set their own titles; i.e., if the
player consistently has an offensive title.  The definition of offensive
is left as an exercise for the reader.

See also: TITLE
#104
NOWIZ

Usage: nowiz

Allows you to hear or ignore messages coming over the wiznet.

See also: WIZNET
#104
OASIS

Oasis is the name of the OLC (On-Line Creation) package that we
use on Phoenix MUD.  OasisOLC is made up of several different,
independent editing modules, plus an informational command. All
of the editor modules share a similar interface, making it easier
to learn the OLC system.

These modules are:

Redit--The room editor
Zedit--The zone editor
Medit--The mob editor
Oedit--The object editor
Sedit--The shop editor
Gedit--The guild editor
olc--An informational command

Before you can begin using OLC to create areas online, you must ask
a god of Implementor level or higher to create you a new zone and
set your permissions to edit that zone.  The system is designed so
that you can only edit the zone to which you have permissions for.
You can currently have permissions to edit up to five zones per
character.  Unless you are working on a special project for an 
implementor, it is rare that you would have permissions on any more
than one or two zones.  If you wish to have more than one zone to 
edit at a time, ask an imp and your situation will be decided on a 
case by case basis.

Note to Implementors:  Imps do not need to have their permissions
set and are able to edit any zone on the mud.  To set another god's
OLC permissions, use the following command:

> set player olc <zone number>

All of the OLC modules use the same editor as is used for boards
and mudmail.  If you need help using the editor, please reference
EDITOR for help on that subject.  For the rest of this help file, the
term editor will refer to an OLC editor, such as medit, sedit, etc.

The OLC system is menu-based.  What this means is that once you
begin editing a particular room, mob, object, etc. you will be
taken out of normal game play and placed into an editor, where 
you can complete all aspects of that item before returning to the 
actual game play.  You will not be able to execute any other mud
commands and you will be completely cut off from communications.  
This is to allow you to work in peace without the constant 
disturbances of the game itself.

A general note regarding saving: As you leave each editor you will be
asked if you wish to save the item internally.  This does NOT mean
that your work has been saved to disk.  You must execute the following
command:

> ?edit save <your zone number>

Where the ? would be replaced by the letter representing what editor
you are using.  (redit, sedit, etc).

It is a good idea to save your work to disk often.  If you cannot
remember if you have saved your work, the OLC command will inform
you which items have not been saved to disk.

See Also: REDIT ZEDIT GEDIT MEDIT OEDIT SEDIT OLC EDITOR
#104
OLC

Usage: olc

This is an informational command which will show you which aspects
of the world have been saved internally, but not yet saved to disk.
You must use the save commands within each editor to save your work
to disk.  See the help on each portion of the OLC package for more
information.

See also: OASIS REDIT ZEDIT MEDIT GEDIT OEDIT SEDIT
#104
OEDIT

Usage:

> oedit <virtual obj num>         
Edit/create object with the virtual number specified

> oedit save <zone>
Save all the object in zone to disk

> oedit save all
Save all object files which have been edited to disk.

Oedit is the object editor portion of OLC.  Oedit allows you to edit
all aspects of objects in the game.  You will only be allowed to
edit/create objects in the zone for which you have permissions.
Please consult your builder's handbook for explanations of individual
fields and flags.

See Also: OASIS OLC MEDIT GEDIT REDIT SEDIT ZEDIT EDITOR
#104
PAGE

Usage: page <player | all> <message>

PAGE is used to send a message, along with a beep, to another player.  Use
PAGE ALL to send a message and a beep to everyone in the game.

Examples:

  > page rasmussen Hey, are you still at the keyboard?
  > page all GAME WILL BE SHUTTING DOWN IN 10 MINUTES

Do not abuse this command.

See Also: PAGEOK
#0
PARDON

Usage: pardon <victim>

Removes killer and thief flags from a player.
#104
PEACE

Usage: peace

This command will stop all combat in a room.
#104
PLAYERS

Usage: players

This command will list, in columnnar format, all of the players
in the playerfile.  The command lists player names, level, site 
where they last logged in from, and the size of their bank accounts.
The data is updated either every reboot or when the player next logs
in to the mud.
#104
POOFIN POOFOUT

Usage: poofin <message>
       poofout <message>

Sets the message seen by players when you use GOTO to move to a different
room.  POOFIN with no argument resets your poofin to the default "appears with
an ear-splitting bang"; POOFOUT with no argument defaults to "disappears in
a puff of smoke."

See also: GOTO
#104
PS DELAY QKILL

Usage: ps
       delay <time to delay> <target for command> <command for target to execute>
       qkill <job number>

PhoenixMUD uses a queue to store and execute various commands and functions.
Things on timers, such as tics and teleport functions will often appear in the
queue when viewed with PS.  Also, with the queue system, you can enter nearly any
command into the queue to be executed at a later time.  This is accomplished
using the DELAY command.  In order to kill a command in the queue, use PS to find
the command's job number, and then use QKILL with that job number to kill the 
command.

Example:
 
>delay 20 swordmaster gos This is a test of the Queue
--This will cause the first instance of 'swordmaster' to gossip, "This is a
  test of the Queue" 20 seconds from now.
>ps
--Find your job in the queue list. Note the job number.
>qkill 5
--Kill job 5 in the queue.

Due to the nature of this command, any abuse of the command queue will                                 
NOT be tolerated, and will result in the removal of the command
privelages at the least.
 
See Also: FORCE
#104
PURGE

Usage: purge [name]

Purge destroys things.  If used with no arguments, it will clear a room of all
mobiles and objects, but will not harm players.  If the argument is an object,
that object (and all of its contents, if any) will be destroyed.  If the
argument is a mobile, the mobile will be destroyed, leaving its inventory and
equipment lying on the ground.

If the argument is the name of a player, the player's inventory and equipment
will drop on the ground and the player will lose his/her connection.  For this
reason, PURGE should only be used on players in disciplinary situations.

Examples:

  > purge
  > purge Pom
  > purge sword
  > purge dragon

See also: DC
#104
REDIT

Usage:

> redit                           
Edit the room you are standing in.

> redit <virtual room num>
Edit/create room of <virtual room number>.

> redit save <zone>               
Save all the rooms in zone to disk.

> redit save all
Save all the room files which have been edited to disk.

Redit is the room editor portion of OLC.  Redit allows you to edit
all aspects of a room in the mud.  You may only edit/create rooms
in the zone for which you have permissions.  Please consult your
builder's handbook for explanations of the meanings of individual
fields and flags.

See Also: OASIS OLC GEDIT OEDIT MEDIT SEDIT ZEDIT EDITOR
#104
RELOAD

Usage: reload < '*' | all | file >

Reload is used to reload text files such as the MOTD from disk into memory.
reload * reloads all the text files below.

Valid files are:

  areas      (/mud_dir/lib/text/areas)
  background (/mud_dir/lib/text/background)
  credits    (/mud_dir/lib/text/credits)
  handbook   (/mud_dir/lib/text/handbook)
  help       (/mud_dir/lib/text/help)
  immlist    (/mud_dir/lib/text/immlist)
  imotd      (/mud_dir/lib/text/imotd)
  news       (/mud_dir/lib/text/news)
  motd       (/mud_dir/lib/text/motd)
  info       (/mud_dir/lib/text/info)
  policy     (/mud_dir/lib/text/policies)
  skills     (/mud_dir/lib/text/skills)
  spells     (/mud_dir/lib/text/spells)
  wizlist    (/mud_dir/lib/text/wizlist)
  xhelp      (/mud_dir/lib/text/help/*)
#104
REROLL

Usage: reroll <player>

REROLL gives a player new stats (i.e., Str, Int, Wis, Dex, Con, and Cha)

See also: STAT
#104
RESTORE

Usage: restore <player | mobile>

RESTORE restores a player or mobile to full hit, mana, and movement points.
If used on immortals, it also sets all skill levels to 100%.
#104
RETURN

Usage: return

RETURN returns you to your regular body if you are switched into a mob.

See also: SWITCH
#104
RLIST OLIST MLIST PLIST TLIST
Usage: rlist <Beginning Vnum> <Ending Vnum>
       rlist -h
       olist <Beginning Vnum> <Ending Vnum>
       mlist <Beginning Vnum> <Ending Vnum>
       plist <Beginning Vnum> <Ending Vnum>
       tlist <Beginning Vnum> <Ending Vnum>

These five commands will list all of the rooms, objects, mobs, paths and 
triggers in the range of vnums specified.  This is a useful command for 
builders who wish to check what mobs, objects, or rooms they have already 
created.  Each command lists several pieces of information about each room, 
object, mob, or path.

If one of these commands is used without the Ending Vnum, the mud assumes
a range of 100 rooms, objects, mobs, or paths.  Thus, if you type rlist 3000, 
the mud will interpret this as rlist 3000 3099.

rlist -h will show a short help screen which describes what each abbreviation
in the listing means.

See also: SHOW
#104
ROOMFLAGS

Usage: roomflags

Toggles a flag which causes the virtual room number and room flags, if any,
to be displayed next to room names.
#104
SEDIT

Usage:

> sedit <virtual shop num>
Edit/create shop with <virtual shop number>.

> sedit save <zone>
Save all shops in zone to disk.

> sedit save all
Save all the shop files which have been edited to disk.

Sedit is the Shop editor portion of OLC.  Sedit allows you to edit
all aspects of shops.  Note that you may only create/edit shops in
the zone for which you have permissions.  Please consult the builder's
handbook for explanations of individual fields and flags.

See Also: OASIS OLC GEDIT OEDIT MEDIT REDIT ZEDIT EDITOR
#104
SET

Usage: set [ file | player ] <character> <field> <value>

SET is an extremely powerful command, capable of setting dozens of aspects of
characters, both players and mobiles.

SET PLAYER forces set to look for a player and not a mobile; useful for
players with names such as 'guard'.

SET FILE lets you change players who are not logged on.  If you use SET FILE
on a player who IS logged on, your change will be lost.  If you wish to set
a player who is in the game but is linkless, use set twice -- once with the
FILE argument, and once without -- to make sure that the change takes.

For toggled fields (BINARY), the value must be ON, OFF, YES, or NO.

The following are valid fields:

Field          Level Required   Who     Value Type   Decription
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
brief          LVL_GOD          PC      BINARY       Brief mode
invstart       LVL_GOD          PC      BINARY       Invisible @ Start
title          LVL_GOD          PC      MISC         Player's title
nosummon       LVL_GRGOD        PC      BINARY       NOSUMMON flag
maxhit         LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Max HP
maxmana        LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Max mana
maxmove        LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Max movement
hit            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Current HP
mana           LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Current mana
move           LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Current movement
align          LVL_GOD          BOTH    NUMBER       Alignment
str            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Strength
stradd         LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Additional STR (18/xx)
int            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Inteligence
wis            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Wisdom
dex            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Dexterity
con            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Constitution
cha            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Charisma
sex            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    MISC         Sex
ac             LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Armor class
gold           LVL_GOD          BOTH    NUMBER       Gold (On hand)
bank           LVL_GOD          PC      NUMBER       Gold in bank
exp            LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Experience points
hitroll        LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       To-Hit modifier
damroll        LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    NUMBER       Damage modifier
invis          LVL_IMPL         PC      NUMBER       Invisible (level)
nohassle       LVL_GRGOD        PC      BINARY       NOHASSLE flag
frozen         LVL_FREEZE       PC      BINARY       FROZEN flag
practices      LVL_GRGOD        PC      NUMBER       # of practices
lessons        LVL_GRGOD        PC      NUMBER       # of practices
drunk          LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    MISC         Drunkeness
hunger         LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    MISC         Hrs to hunger
thirst         LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    MISC         Hrs to thirst
killer         LVL_GOD          PC      BINARY       KILLER flag
thief          LVL_GOD          PC      BINARY       THIEF flag
level          LVL_IMPL         BOTH    NUMBER       Level
room           LVL_IMPL         BOTH    NUMBER       Move to room
roomflag       LVL_GRGOD        PC      BINARY       ROOMFLAGS flag
siteok         LVL_GRGOD        PC      BINARY       SITEOK flag
deleted        LVL_IMPL         PC      BINARY       DELETED flag
class          LVL_GRGOD        BOTH    MISC         Class
race	       LVL_GRGOD	PC	MISC	     Race
nowizlist      LVL_GOD          PC      BINARY       Not Wizlistable flag
quest          LVL_GOD          PC      BINARY       QUESTING flag
loadroom       LVL_GRGOD        PC      MISC         Room to load in (#/off)
color          LVL_GOD          PC      BINARY       COLOR flag
idnum          LVL_IMPL         PC      NUMBER       ID Number
passwd         LVL_IMPL         PC      MISC         Player password
nodelete       LVL_GOD          PC      BINARY       Undeletable flag
olc	       LVL_IMPL		PC	NUMBER	     Sets the zone which
						     the god may edit.

See also: STAT
