#104
SHOW

Usage: show <mode> [argument]

Valid Modes:
active           badspllvl         buffer           
clan             connections       death
errors           fighting          godcommand
godrooms         guildmaster       hometowns
houses           idle              itemspell
kills            logbuf            maze 
mobs             mobspecs          newbieeq
nomagic          nomob             noposchk
norecall         nosummon          notrack
objs             olczones          peaceful
pkill            plague            player
private          rconnect          regen   
rent             shops             skills
spells           stats             teleport
tunnel           twohanded         zones            

The SHOW command displays information.  Some modes of show require additional
information, such as a player name.

     active: Shows all active zones.
  badspllvl: Shows potions, scrolls, staffs, wands, and pills with bad
             spell level numbers.
     buffer: Shows all buffers currently in use in the mud.
       clan: Shows all rooms with the roomflag CLAN.
connections: Shows connections to and from a zone.
      death: Shows all death traps in the game.
     errors: Shows all rooms which have exits to room 0.
   fighting: Shows all combatants in the game.
 godcommand: Shows all god commands and their levels.
   godrooms: Shows all rooms in the world which have the flag GODROOM.
guildmaster: Shows the list of all guilds in the game.
  hometowns: Shows the list of all hometowns in the game.
     houses: Shows the houses that are currently defined.
       idle: Shows all of the zones which have idled out.
  itemspell: Shows all items with a particular spell.
      kills: Shows the list of recently killed mobs for a player.
     logbuf: Writes the contents of Show Buffers to the syslog.
       maze: Displays a map of a maze zone.
       mobs: Shows all of the mobs for the zone which you are standing in.
             Superceded by MLIST.
   mobspecs: Shows the list of spec procs available for use by builders.
   newbieeq: Shows all equipment in the game with the flag NEWBIE.
    nomagic: Shows all rooms with the roomflag NOMAGIC.
      nomob: Shows all rooms with the roomflag NOMOB.
   noposchk: Shows all equipment in the game with the !POSCHK flag.
   norecall: Shows all rooms with the roomflag NORECALL.
   nosummon: Shows all rooms with the roomflag NOSUMMON.
    notrack: Shows all rooms with the roomflag NOTRACK.
       objs: Shows all of the objs for the zone which you are standing in.
             Superceded by OLIST.
   olczones: Shows which zones an immortal has permissions to edit.
   peaceful: Shows all rooms with the roomflag PEACEFUL.
      pkill: Shows all rooms with the roomflag PKILL.
     plague: Shows all mobs and players affected by Plague.
     player: Shows player summary information, simply provide a player name.
    private: Shows all rooms with the roomflag PRIVATE.
   rconnect: Shows all connections to and from a room.
      regen: Shows all rooms with the roomflag REGEN.
       rent: Shows the filename and contents of a player's rent file.
      shops: Shows all the shops in the game and their buy/sell parameters.
     skills: Shows the skills and % known for a player.
     spells: Shows the spells and % known for a player.
      stats: Shows game status information including players in game, mobs etc.
     tunnel: Shows all rooms with the roomflag TUNNEL.
   teleport: Shows all teleport rooms in the game. 
    twohand: Shows all equipment in the game with the flag TWO_HAND.
      zones: Shows all the zones in the game and their current reset status.
             An age of -1 means it is in the 'to be reset next' queue.

See also: STAT ZRESET MLIST OLIST
#104
SHUTDOWN

Usage: shutdown [reboot | die | pause]

SHUTDOWN shuts the MUD down.  The SHUTDOWN command works in conjunction with
CircleMUD's 'autorun' script.  If you are not using autorun, the arguments are
meaningless.  If you are using autorun, the following arguments are available:

REBOOT     Pause only 5 seconds instead of the normal 40 before trying to
           restart the MUD.

DIE        Kill the autorun script; the MUD will not reboot until autorun is
           explicitly run again.

PAUSE      Create a file called 'paused' in Circle's root directory; do not
           try to restart the MUD until 'paused' is removed.
#104
SKILLSET

Usage: skillset <name> '<skill>' <value>

Sets the specified skill to the specified value.

See also: SET
#104
SLAY

Usage: slay <victim>

The slay command allows gods of Implementor level to kill a victim instantly.
When a player is slain they are dropped back to the game menu.  The victim's
corpse, with equipment is left in the room in which they were slain.  The victim
does not lose any experience from being slain.  If the victim is a mob, the mob
is killed instantly, and its corpse left on the ground.
#104
SLOWNS

Usage: slowns

Toggles whether or not IP addresses are resolved into readable site names.
If slow nameserver is enabled, USERS will show only IP addresses.
(128.123.222.111 etc).

See also: USERS
#104
SNOOP

Usage: snoop [player]

Snoop allows you to listen in on another player's session; everything the
player sees or types will be displayed on your terminal preceded by a '%'.

Obviously, there are many ethical considerations to the use of this command; 
snoop should be used minimally, and only for disciplinary purposes.

Type snoop <your name> or just snoop to stop snooping.
#104
SNOWBALL

Snowball is just a social -- it prints a funny message, and nothing more.
#0
STAT

Usage: stat [player | object | mobile | file] <name>

Gives information about players, monsters, and objects in the game.  The type
argument is optional.

STAT PLAYER will search only for players; useful for statting people with
names such as Red or Cityguard.

STAT OBJECT will search only for objects.

STAT MOBILE will search only for monsters.

STAT FILE is used to stat players who are not logged in; the information
displayed comes from the playerfile.

Examples:

  > stat fido
  > stat player red
  > stat mobile red
  > stat file niandra
  > stat object thunderbolt

See also: VSTAT
#104
STRING

Usage: string <type> <name> <field> [<string> | <keyword>]

For changing the text-strings associated with objects and characters.  The
format is:

Type is either 'obj' or 'char'.

Field is one of the following(the names may be abbreviated):

Name                  (the call-name of an obj/char - kill giant)
Short                 (for inventory lists (obj's) and actions (char's))
Long                  (for when obj/character is seen in room)
Title                 (for players)
Description           (For look at.  For obj's, must be followed by a keyword)
Delete-description    (only for obj's. Must be followed by keyword)

Where a string is expected as the last argument, a newline will make you enter
a string mode, and the game will prompt you for new lines to the string until
you exceed the maximum length for the string or you end a line with a '@'.

Mobiles and objects currently cannot be strung.
#104
SWITCH

Usage: switch <monster>

SWITCH is used to take over the body of mobiles; useful for interactive
adventures.  If the monster in which you are switched is killed you will be
returned to your own body.

See also: RETURN
#104
SYSLOG

Usage: syslog [off | brief | normal | complete]

The SYSLOG command controls how detailed of an on-line system log you see.
Messages surrounded by '[' and ']' are syslog messages and are in green
if you have color on.

The messages you receive are level dependent.

Examples:

  > syslog complete
  > syslog off

See also: COLOR
#104
UNAFFECT

Usage: unaffect <victim>

Removes all affections (i.e., spell effects) from a player.

See also: STAT
#104
UNUSED

Usage: unused [class]

Displays a list of spells, skills, or 'other powers' which are currently not
used or do not fall into other categories.

See Also: SKILLS SPELLS
#104
UPTIME

Usage: uptime

Displays when the game was booted and calculates how long ago that was.
#104
USERS

Usage: users [switches]

USERS gives a list of all sockets (i.e., connections) currently active on the
MUD.  The multi-column display shows the socket number (used by DC), class,
level, and name of the player connected, connection state, idle time, and
hostname.

The following switches are available:

-k or -o   Show only outlaws (killers and thieves).
-p         Show only sockets in the playing sockets.
-d         Show only non-playing (deadweight) sockets.
-l min-max Show only sockets whose characters are from level min to max.
-n <name>  Show the socket with <name> associated with it.
-h <host>  Show all sockets from <host>.
-c list    Show only sockets whose characters' classes are in list.

See also: DC, SLOWNS
#104
VNUM

Usage: vnum <mob | obj> <name>

Gives a list of the virtual numbers of objects or mobiles which have the
specified name.

Examples:

  > vnum obj ring
  1. [  904] a platinum ring
  2. [ 2543] a seashell ring
  3. [ 2588] a small emerald ring
  
  > vnum mob dragon
  1. [    1] Puff
  2. [  908] the dragon turtle
  3. [ 2548] the Master of Illusions
#104
VSTAT

Usage: vstat < mob | obj > <virtual number>

Used to stat a prototypical mobile or object by virtual number.

Examples:

  > vstat obj 3000
  > vstat mob 3001

See also: LOAD, STAT, VNUM
#104
VBUGS VCHANGES VTYPOS VIDEAS VSYSLOG VSCRIPTERR VGOLD
Usage: vbugs
       vchanges
       vtypos
       videas
       vsyslog
       vscripterr
       vgold

These commands are used to view various text files in the game.  The bugs, typos, and
ideas files are the results of use of the commands of the same name.  The vchanges file
displays the list of changes made to the code by the Phoenix coders.  The vsyslog command 
displays the syslog for the mud.  The vscripterr log is a list of the dg_script errors
since the last mud reboot.  In vgold is shown the monetary transfers that have taken
place.

See also: BUG IDEA TYPO GODLOG
#104
VBIGRENT VDELETE VLASTCMD VBUF VERRORS VMAILLOG VGODCMD VRIP VCORPSE VDELETED

Usage: vbigrent         vmaillog
       vbuf             vlastcmd 
       vdelete          vrip
       verrors          vgodcmd
       vcorpse		vdeleted

When the mud runs, it creates a syslog of important events.  This log is stored as a text
file and parts of it can be viewed with the VSYSLOG command.  Whenever the mud reboots
or crashes, the syslog is parsed for certain keywords and the info stored in seperate files
to keep all relevant information together.  Some of these files can be viewed with the above
commands.   Most are self-explanatory, but just in case, here is what each does:
	bigrent--stores info about those who rent with more than 200 items.
        maillog--currently unimplemented.
        buf--stores buffer information.  Only useful to a coder.
	lastcmd--stores the last command used before a crash.  Useful to a coder.
	delete--shows player deletions.
	rip--shows player deaths.
	errors--shows mud SYSERRS, some of which are building related.
	godcmd--shows the god command log.
	corpse--shows the corpse looting log.
	deleted--shows the self delete log.

See Also: VSYSLOG
#104
VWEAR

Usage: vwear [value]

VWEAR returns a list of all of the equipment in the game for the body
position specified by 'value'.  Typing VWEAR without an argument returns
a list of all of the valid body part values which can be used with the
command.  Some of the equipment lists returned by VWEAR can be quite large.
#104
WIZHELP

Usage: wizhelp

Gives a list of the special commands available at your level.
#104
WIZLOCK

Usage: wizlock [value]

WIZLOCK allows you to restrict the game.  WIZLOCK 1 prevents new characters
from being created.  WIZLOCK n, 2 <= n <= 34, prevents new characters and
allows only characters level n and above to log on.  WIZLOCK with no argument
displays the current WIZLOCK level.

See also: BAN
#104
WIZNET ;

The immortal communication channel.

Usage: wiznet [ '@' | '+' | '-' | '#' ] [<text>]

Examples:

  >; <text>    - just sends text
  >; @         - shows all gods that are on and visible to you
               - also shows if the gods who are visible to you are writing

This code was provided by Gnort.

See also: NOWIZ
#104
ZEDIT

Usage:

> zedit
Edit the zone info for the room you are standing in.

> zedit <virtual room num>
Edit the zone info for <virtual room num>

> zedit save <zone>
Save all the zone info for that zone to disk.

> zedit save all
Save all the zone files which have been edited to disk.

> zedit new <zone>
Implementor command only.  Create a new zone with specified zone number.

Zedit allows the creation and editing of zones.  The zone information is
what ties the whole area together, specifying what gets loaded where and
which doors to close and lock.  Only gods of Implementor level may create
zones.  Builders may edit the zone information only in the zone for which
they have permissions.  Please consult your builder's handbook for more
information on explanations of individual flags and fields.

See Also: OASIS OLC GEDIT OEDIT MEDIT REDIT SEDIT EDITOR
#104
ZRESET

Usage: zreset < zone | '*' | '.' >

Used to force a zone to reset.  '*' forces all zones to reset.  '.' forces
the zone you are in to reset.

See also: SHOW -> ZONES IDLE
#104
